WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
There is a small canal, it is a remnant of this hyaloid vessel and a fine canal

2
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.480
currently remains in the eye

3
00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:17.350
which leads us directly to a primary axis important which is called Cloquet's canal.

4
00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:25.660
And you see it here, you see, it's a remnant in this humor

5
00:00:28.791 --> 00:00:33.571
you have different humors this is the humor aqueous and then the vitreous humor and

6
00:00:33.571 --> 00:00:37.591
the vitreous humor you really have to imagine that it's like a balloon

7
00:00:41.351 --> 00:00:48.711
of gelatinous water that is in the eye that floats of water with at its center a fine canal

8
00:00:52.211 --> 00:00:54.841
which comes directly onto the lens here

9
00:00:56.142 --> 00:01:01.902
You see, here, which is a direct axis with the optic nerve, or the hyaloid canal.

10
00:01:06.542 --> 00:01:10.242
So the lens, it comes from your skin, surface ectoderm.

11
00:01:14.502 --> 00:01:20.392
Invagination, fifth week, lens vesicle is surrounded by vascularized mesenchyme.

12
00:01:21.422 --> 00:01:23.302
Then, I have this connection.

13
00:01:24.213 --> 00:01:28.483
Juxtacrine, the placode invaginates and will become the lens.

14
00:01:28.483 --> 00:01:32.133
It's the same movement as a neural tube. Poof, touc!

15
00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:36.093
Inside, there remains a space, which we call the crystalline lens.

16
00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:39.694
There is a whole evolution of this crystalline lens which will form

17
00:01:40.193 --> 00:01:43.713
primitive lines, etc. I will skip this part.

18
00:01:43.853 --> 00:01:47.773
It's important to understand how it forms like a neural tube,

19
00:01:47.773 --> 00:01:50.023
and it becomes here, on its own.

20
00:01:50.023 --> 00:01:52.093
It is truly the lens of the eye.

21
00:01:53.324 --> 00:01:57.014
You see here the colobomic fissure; there is a line here below,

22
00:01:57.014 --> 00:02:00.554
you see, and that's what will later form, with the arteries,

23
00:02:00.554 --> 00:02:01.824
the canal of Cloquet.

24
00:02:04.184 --> 00:02:07.954
It will close with the hyaloid artery and vein, which is central;

25
00:02:07.954 --> 00:02:11.624
this important artery, this is a colobomic fissure in the eye

26
00:02:15.564 --> 00:02:17.244
that is not closed.

27
00:02:21.375 --> 00:02:22.295
What is this?

28
00:02:22.295 --> 00:02:27.555
It is the primary lens vesicle which becomes a secondary lens vesicle with the lens

29
00:02:27.555 --> 00:02:29.765
placode; this is the crystalline lens,

30
00:02:34.595 --> 00:02:37.475
juxtacrine, integration, you see.

31
00:02:46.815 --> 00:02:47.805
With the layer.

32
00:02:47.805 --> 00:02:53.175
Here, which will form what we call, externally, it is the sclera, the first coat.

33
00:02:58.226 --> 00:03:02.306
The second coat is here, all of this, in fact, is the choroid

34
00:03:07.346 --> 00:03:10.456
which will form the attachment with the crystalline lens.

35
00:03:11.686 --> 00:03:14.866
Anterior chamber, posterior chamber in the part.

36
00:03:16.497 --> 00:03:19.977
anterior. So I can divide the eye into parts.

37
00:03:20.757 --> 00:03:26.477
forward and backward, and in the anterior part, I divide it into anterior chambers

38
00:03:26.477 --> 00:03:27.877
and posterior chambers.

39
00:03:27.877 --> 00:03:30.037
Then, we have what is called the

40
00:03:32.517 --> 00:03:34.597
conjunctiva, the cornea,

41
00:03:37.497 --> 00:03:40.217
which is in continuity, conjunctiva,

42
00:03:42.497 --> 00:03:46.627
cornea, and the cornea is in continuity. with the sclera.

43
00:03:48.408 --> 00:03:52.768
And what do we call the part here, the second, we called it the choroid.

44
00:03:59.128 --> 00:04:00.638
We call it the uvea.

45
00:04:00.638 --> 00:04:04.728
And the uvea, it comes from the Latin uva, which means grape.

46
00:04:07.328 --> 00:04:11.298
This small membrane, like this, is a membrane very rich in vessels.

47
00:04:11.298 --> 00:04:12.606
It divides into three.

48
00:04:12.639 --> 00:04:17.919
parts, or into two or three major parts, which we call the choroid here,

49
00:04:17.919 --> 00:04:18.949
and anteriorly

50
00:04:21.139 --> 00:04:24.059
the cornea, and the cornea is transparent.

51
00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:31.759
But here posteriorly, there will be the iris, which is a continuity of the choroid, and

52
00:04:35.919 --> 00:04:38.559
we will have here connections, a ligament.

53
00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:45.300
which we call the suspensory ligament of the iris, which maintains the iris,

54
00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.070
with small muscles called the ciliary muscles,

55
00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:51.060
which will...

56
00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:56.750
We will see that in a bit more detail later. So the retina, in fact,

57
00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:59.510
is the innermost layer of the eye.

58
00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:03.410
Do you remember this first vesicle, this small sphere that emerges,

59
00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:05.760
which we call the primary optic vesicle.

60
00:05:08.781 --> 00:05:13.421
which will make a movement like this, you see, like a calyx, a vessel.

61
00:05:15.321 --> 00:05:19.161
It is made of two layers, an inner layer and an outer layer.

62
00:05:20.701 --> 00:05:24.601
And this is where one understands what is called a retinal detachment.

